@page "/scoped-dbcontext-repro"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveServerRenderMode(prerender: false))

<PageTitle>Scoped DbContext repro</PageTitle>

<h1>Scoped DbContext repro</h1>

<p class="lead">
    Every card below is its own component. Each one calls the same scoped user service from
    <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>, and that scoped service holds the same scoped EF Core <code>AppDbContext</code>.
</p>

<div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
    Expected result: one or more cards should show EF Core's
    <code>A second operation was started on this context instance...</code> message because the components overlap on
    the same <code>DbContext</code>.
</div>

<div class="card border-warning shadow-sm mb-4">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h2 class="h4">Why this happens</h2>

        <p>
            The important detail is that in <strong>Blazor Server</strong>, a <strong>scoped</strong> service is usually
            scoped to the user's <strong>circuit</strong>, not to one component. That means all of the components on this
            page can receive the <strong>same</strong> <code>IScopedUserService</code> instance.
        </p>

        <p>
            In this demo, that shared service also holds a shared scoped <code>AppDbContext</code>. When several
            components start loading at nearly the same time, they all call into the same service, which means they all
            try to use the same <code>DbContext</code> instance at the same time.
        </p>

        <p>
            EF Core <code>DbContext</code> is <strong>not thread-safe</strong> and does <strong>not</strong> allow
            multiple operations to run on the same instance concurrently. As soon as one query is still in flight and
            another component starts a second query on that same instance, EF Core throws the concurrency exception.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h5 mt-4">So who is "at fault"?</h3>

        <p class="mb-2">
            Usually it is <strong>not just one thing</strong>. The failure comes from this combination:
        </p>

        <ol class="mb-3">
            <li>Blazor Server shares scoped services across components in the same circuit.</li>
            <li>The user service stores a scoped <code>DbContext</code>, so that context is shared too.</li>
            <li>Multiple components call the service concurrently from <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>.</li>
            <li>EF Core rejects concurrent use of one <code>DbContext</code> instance.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            So the real issue is <strong>concurrent use of one shared <code>DbContext</code> instance</strong>. The
            service lifetime and the injected <code>DbContext</code> lifetime are both part of the chain, but the
            actual rule being broken is: <strong>one <code>DbContext</code> instance cannot handle overlapping
            operations</strong>.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Put another way: the scoped service is not automatically wrong, and dependency injection is not broken. The
            problematic pattern is keeping one scoped <code>DbContext</code> inside a shared scoped service and then
            calling it from several components at once.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<p>
    If the exception does not appear on the first load, refresh the page. The demo already adds a small alignment
    delay and a command interceptor delay to make the overlap more likely.
</p>

